virglrenderer: Venus descriptor sets and pipeline layouts stay inside what the driver sized

What: vkr keeps a table of each descriptor set layout (bindings sorted by
number: type, count, flags, stages, immutable samplers and their planes, a
mutable binding's types), shared by the layout, its sets and the pipeline
layouts that use it (refcounted: a layout may be destroyed while they live),
and checks against it: layout creates (binding numbers below 2^16, known
types, inline blocks within maxInlineUniformBlockSize, at most 2^24
descriptors, dynamic buffers within the device's limits, binding flags for
every binding or none, the variable count on the largest binding, non-NULL
immutable samplers, push descriptor layouts within maxPushDescriptors, a type
list for every mutable binding), pools (known types, at most 2^24 per size
and sets), set allocation (one variable count per set, within the binding's),
writes and copies (the binding exists, the type is its own, the elements lie
in it and the consecutive bindings the spec allows, those without descriptors
and numbers the layout lacks skipped, from an element that may lie past the
first binding; an update over several bindings goes to the driver as one write
or copy per binding, each part checked in its own binding: type, mutable type
list, YCbCr planes; inline data of the written size;
samplers present unless immutable; NULL views, buffers (dynamic ones too)
and texel views only with nullDescriptor; buffer ranges inside the buffer and
maxUniform/StorageBufferRange; a multi-planar view only where immutable YCbCr
samplers sized the binding), pipeline layouts (set layouts within
maxBoundDescriptorSets and not NULL, push constant ranges inside the limit
with one range per stage, dynamic buffers within the limits), and
vkCmdBindDescriptorSets(2) and vkCmdPushDescriptorSet(2) (sets inside the
pipeline layout, each with the pipeline layout's dynamic buffers there, one
offset per dynamic buffer; a push to a push descriptor set layout, its writes
as above). Image views and samplers keep their plane counts. A refusal ends
the app's context (the guest makes and updates these without waiting).
Why: KosmicKrisp (NDEBUG) writes a whole write at its first binding's offset +
element * that binding's stride, and MoltenVK with that binding's layout, but the
strides and layouts of consecutive bindings of one type differ (immutable YCbCr
samplers of 2 or 3 planes, a mutable binding as wide as its largest type): a
legal write over a 3-plane and a 1-plane binding landed past the set.
KosmicKrisp sizes a layout's binding array by the largest binding
number + 1 (0xffffffff wraps to 0, then writes binding[0xffffffff]), counts
dynamic buffers in a uint8 into a 64-bit bitset, sizes layouts, sets and pools
in 32-bit arithmetic, writes each descriptor at the binding's offset + element *
stride into the set's memory (assert compiled out), dereferences a NULL
sampler, writes a descriptor per plane of a multi-planar view, and reads
pDynamicOffsets by the layouts' counts and set->dynamic_buffers by the pipeline
layout's; Mesa's vk_pipeline_layout copies set layouts into an array of 32 and
push constant ranges into one of 14. MoltenVK writes at the binding's offset
too, and ends the process on a write of another type. Probed on Apple7:
SIGBUS/SIGSEGV 3 of 3 (ADR 0065).
Upstream: OmacVM policy (spec VUIDs), draft only.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-commands.c (the d- and p-layout cases and the
c-sets/c-push-set cases); Tests/virgl/test-venus-descriptor-walk.c (the walk over
consecutive bindings against a model of the spec's rule, on the CPU, seeds 1, 2,
3, 77 and 12345); --time: us per vkUpdateDescriptorSets (1 and 1000 writes) and
vkCmdBindDescriptorSets through vkr.

